NNAAMMEE
     mmddccoonnffiigg — create and control memory disks

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmddccoonnffiigg --aa --tt _t_y_p_e [--nn] [--oo [nnoo]_o_p_t_i_o_n] ... [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--ss _s_i_z_e]
              [--SS _s_e_c_t_o_r_s_i_z_e] [--uu _u_n_i_t] [--xx _s_e_c_t_o_r_s_/_t_r_a_c_k] [--yy _h_e_a_d_s_/_c_y_l_i_n_d_e_r]
              [--LL _l_a_b_e_l]
     mmddccoonnffiigg --dd --uu _u_n_i_t [--oo [nnoo]_f_o_r_c_e]
     mmddccoonnffiigg --rr --uu _u_n_i_t --ss _s_i_z_e [--oo [nnoo]_f_o_r_c_e]
     mmddccoonnffiigg --ll [--nn] [--vv] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--uu _u_n_i_t]
     mmddccoonnffiigg _f_i_l_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmddccoonnffiigg utility creates and controls md(4) devices.

     Options indicate an action to be performed:

     --aa      Attach a memory disk.  This will configure and attach a memory
             disk with the parameters specified and attach it to the system.
             If the --uu _u_n_i_t option is not provided, the newly created device
             name will be printed on stdout.

     --dd      Detach a memory disk from the system and release all resources.

     --rr      Resize a memory disk.

     --tt _t_y_p_e
             Select the type of the memory disk.

             mmaalllloocc  Storage for this type of memory disk is allocated with
                     malloc(9).  This limits the size to the malloc bucket
                     limit in the kernel.  If the --oo rreesseerrvvee option is not
                     set, creating and filling a large malloc-backed memory
                     disk is a very easy way to panic the system.

             vvnnooddee   A file specified with --ff _f_i_l_e becomes the backing store
                     for this memory disk.

             sswwaapp    Storage for this type of memory disk is allocated from
                     buffer memory.  Pages get pushed out to swap when the
                     system is under memory pressure, otherwise they stay in
                     the operating memory.  Using sswwaapp backing is generally
                     preferred instead of using mmaalllloocc backing.

             nnuullll    Bitsink; all writes do nothing, all reads return zeroes.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Filename to use for the vnode type memory disk.  The --aa and --tt
             vvnnooddee options are implied if not specified.

     --ll      List configured devices.  If given with --uu, display details about
             that particular device.  If given with --ff _f_i_l_e, display md(4) de‐
             vice names of which _f_i_l_e is used as the backing store.  If both
             of --uu and --ff options are specified, display devices which match
             the two conditions.  If the --vv option is specified, show all de‐
             tails.

     --nn      When printing md(4) device names, print only the unit number
             without the md(4) prefix.

     --ss _s_i_z_e
             Size of the memory disk.  _S_i_z_e is the number of 512 byte sectors
             unless suffixed with a bb, kk, mm, gg, tt, or pp which denotes byte,
             kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte and petabyte respectively.
             When used without the --rr option, the --aa and --tt sswwaapp options are
             implied if not specified.

     --SS _s_e_c_t_o_r_s_i_z_e
             Sectorsize to use for the memory disk, in bytes.

     --xx _s_e_c_t_o_r_s_/_t_r_a_c_k
             See the description of the --yy option below.

     --yy _h_e_a_d_s_/_c_y_l_i_n_d_e_r
             For mmaalllloocc or vvnnooddee backed devices, the --xx and --yy options can be
             used to specify a synthetic geometry.  This is useful for con‐
             structing bootable images for later download to other devices.

     --LL _l_a_b_e_l
             Associate a label (arbitrary string) with the new memory disk.
             The label can then be inspected with

                   mmddccoonnffiigg --ll --vv

     --oo [nnoo]_o_p_t_i_o_n
             Set or reset options.

             [nnoo]aassyynncc
                     For vvnnooddee backed devices: avoid IO_SYNC for increased
                     performance but at the risk of deadlocking the entire
                     kernel.

             [nnoo]ccaacchhee
                     For vvnnooddee backed devices: enable/disable caching of data
                     in system caches.  The default is to not cache.

                     Accesses via the device are converted to accesses via the
                     vnode.  The caching policy for the vnode is used ini‐
                     tially.  This is normally to cache.  This caching policy
                     is retained if the ccaacchhee option is used.  Otherwise,
                     caching is limited by releasing data from caches soon af‐
                     ter each access.  The release has the same semantics as
                     the POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED feature of posix_fadvise(2).  The
                     result is that with normal (non-zfs) caching, buffers are
                     released from the buffer cache soon after they are con‐
                     structed, but their data is kept in the page cache at
                     lower priority.

                     The ccaacchhee option tends to waste memory by giving unwanted
                     double caching, but it saves time if there is memory to
                     spare.

             [nnoo]rreesseerrvvee
                     Allocate and reserve all needed storage from the start,
                     rather than as needed.

             [nnoo]cclluusstteerr
                     Enable clustering on this disk.

             [nnoo]ccoommpprreessss
                     Enable/disable compression features to reduce memory us‐
                     age.

             [nnoo]ffoorrccee
                     Disable/enable extra sanity checks to prevent the user
                     from doing something that might adversely affect the sys‐
                     tem.  This can be used with the --dd flag to forcibly de‐
                     stroy an md(4) disk that is still in use.

             [nnoo]mmuussttddeeaalllloocc
                     For vvnnooddee backed devices: detect whether hole-punching is
                     supported by the underlying file system.  If the file
                     system supports hole-punching, then to handle a
                     BIO_DELETE request, some or all of the request's opera‐
                     tion range may be turned into a hole in the file used for
                     backing store.  Any parts which are not turned into holes
                     are zero-filled in the file.  If the file system does not
                     support hole-punching, BIO_DELETE requests to the device
                     are not handled and will fail with EOPNOTSUPP.

                     When mmuussttddeeaalllloocc is not specified or [nnoo]mmuussttddeeaalllloocc is
                     specified, for a BIO_DELETE request, if the file system
                     supports hole-punching, some or all of the request's op‐
                     eration range may be turned into a hole in the file used
                     for backing store.  Any parts which are not turned into
                     holes are zero-filled in the file.  If the file system of
                     the vnode type memory disk does not support hole-punch‐
                     ing, the request's operation range is zero-filled in the
                     file.

             [nnoo]rreeaaddoonnllyy
                     Enable/disable readonly mode.

             [nnoo]vveerriiffyy
                     For vvnnooddee backed devices: enable/disable requesting veri‐
                     fication of the file used for backing store.  The type of
                     verification depends on which security features are
                     available.  One example of verification is testing file
                     integrity with checksums or cryptographic signatures.

     --uu _u_n_i_t
             Request a specific unit number or device name for the md(4) de‐
             vice instead of automatic allocation.  If a device name is speci‐
             fied, it must start with “md” followed by the unit number.

     The last form, mmddccoonnffiigg _f_i_l_e, is provided for convenience as an abbrevia‐
     tion of mmddccoonnffiigg --aa --tt vvnnooddee --ff _f_i_l_e.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Create a disk with _/_t_m_p_/_b_o_o_t_._f_l_p as backing storage.  The name of the al‐
     located unit will be printed on stdout, such as “md0”:

           mdconfig /tmp/boot.flp

     Create a 1 gigabyte swap backed memory disk named “md3”:

           mdconfig -s 1g -u md3

     Detach and free all resources used by _/_d_e_v_/_m_d_3:

           mdconfig -du md3

     Show detailed information on current memory disks:

           mdconfig -lv

     Resize the “md3” memory disk to 2 gigabytes:

           mdconfig -rs 2g -u md3

     Create a 1 gigabyte swap backed disk, initialize an ffs(7) file system on
     it, and mount it on _/_t_m_p:

           mdconfig -s 1g -u md10
           newfs -U /dev/md10
           mount /dev/md10 /tmp
           chmod 1777 /tmp

     Create a memory disk out of an ISO 9660 CD image file, using the first
     available md(4) device, and then mount it:

           mount -t cd9660 /dev/`mdconfig -f cdimage.iso` /mnt

     Create a file-backed device from a hard disk image that begins with 512K
     of raw header information.  gnop(8) is used to skip over the header in‐
     formation, positioning _m_d_1_._n_o_p to the start of the filesystem in the im‐
     age.

           mdconfig -u md1 -f diskimage.img
           gnop create -o 512K md1
           mount /dev/md1.nop /mnt

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     fpathconf(2), fspacectl(2), open(2), md(4), ffs(7), gpart(8), mdmfs(8),
